                ; `kid.box` as pixels, and `resolveUnits` is what puts them
                ; there — the flow calls it on each child just before laying it
                ; out, so a child IS resolved by the time it is measured, and a
                ; GRANDCHILD is not. This pass walks grandchildren.
                ;
                ; The symptom was a container narrower than the things inside
                ; it: a footer's two buttons came to 216 and their row was
                ; sized 164, which is 216 less exactly two buttons' worth of
                ; chrome, and the pair hung out over the right edge of the
                ; card. "Containers leak over" is what a missing padding term
                ; looks like from the outside.
                ;
                ; The basis is this element's own inner box, which is what the
                ; flow will use a moment later. It matters only for a
                ; PERCENTAGE padding, whose max-content size is undefined in
                ; CSS anyway and which nothing in this gallery uses; `px` and
                ; `em` resolve the same whatever they are given.
                ;
                ; AND A MEASUREMENT MUST NOT FREEZE THE RESOLUTION. `el` is
                ; being MEASURED, so its inner width is not decided yet — a
                ; `flex: 1` item is asked for its min-content size in the
                ; middle of the pass that works out how wide it gets, and
                ; `calculatedInnerWidth` is still zero there. Resolving a kid
                ; against that zero is harmless for the measure (a percentage
                ; contributes nothing to min-content, which is what CSS says
                ; too) and fatal afterwards: `resolveUnits` latches
                ; `unitsResolved`, so `width: 100%` stayed at the 0 it was
                ; measured with and the flow never asked again.
